# Arguments for busybox syslogd, sourced by /etc/init.d/S01syslogd (which
# the busybox package installs because its default config carries the
# syslogd applet and no other syslog package is selected).
#
# Where this writes: /var/log/messages, and /var/log is a symlink to ../tmp
# in Buildroot's skeleton, so the log lives on the /tmp tmpfs. That is
# deliberate. The root filesystem is read-only, and the only writable
# partition is /data, which /etc/init.d/S15data does not mount until well
# after S01 - so syslogd cannot write there at the point it starts, and a
# log that survived reboots would mean writing to eMMC or SD continuously
# for the life of the device. Logs are therefore volatile: they cover the
# session you are debugging and are gone at the next boot.
#
# What this file changes is the bound. busybox rotates by default at 200 KB
# keeping one old file, so roughly 400 KB of history - short enough that a
# chatty daemon can push the interesting part out before anyone reads it.
# 512 KB with four rotations gives about 2.5 MB, which on a tmpfs sized at
# half of RAM costs nothing and holds a whole boot plus a session's worth of
# a misbehaving service.
#
#   -s SIZE   max size in KB before rotation (0 disables rotation)
#   -b N      how many rotated logs to keep (max 99, 0 purges instead)
#
# Reading them: "logread" only works with the shared-memory backend, which
# this build does not use (CONFIG_FEATURE_IPC_SYSLOG_BUFFER_SIZE=0), so use
# the files - tail -f /var/log/messages. Kernel messages arrive here too,
# via S02klogd, and are also in dmesg regardless of syslogd.
#
# To keep logs across reboots instead, the sequence is: let this stay as it
# is for early boot, and add a script ordered after S15data that restarts
# syslogd with -O /data/log/messages. Doing it here would silently drop
# every message logged before /data mounts.
SYSLOGD_ARGS="-s 512 -b 4"
